# Latin America batch (1888-2023): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"la188828": dict(
 bluf="Brazilian abolitionists, enslaved people fleeing the plantations and Regent Princess Isabel set out to end slavery in the last country in the Americas that kept it. The Golden Law of 13 May 1888 freed about 700,000 people at once, with no compensation for owners and no land for the freed. Planters withdrew their support from the monarchy, which fell eighteen months later, and Black Brazilians entered freedom at the bottom of the social order.",
 now="Abolition without land or schooling left Black Brazilians at the bottom of the labour market from 1888, and state subsidies for European immigrants kept them there. Brazil has the largest population of African descent outside Africa, and racial gaps in income, education and policing remain wide.",
 story="Brazil had received more enslaved Africans than any other country and still held about 700,000 people in slavery in 1888. A law of 1871 had freed children born to enslaved mothers, and a law of 1885 freed the elderly. Through the 1880s enslaved people left the plantations in large numbers and the army declined to hunt them. On 13 May 1888 Princess Isabel, acting as regent for Brazilian Emperor Pedro II, signed the Lei Áurea, the Golden Law, abolishing slavery without compensation.",
 why="Mass flight from the plantations made slavery unworkable by 1887, and the army's refusal to recapture runaways removed the means of enforcing it. A national abolitionist movement had made slavery a liability for the crown. The laws of 1871 and 1885 had already set a closing date in all but name.",
 then="Planters who had lost their workforce without compensation backed the officers who overthrew Brazilian Emperor Pedro II in November 1889. The freed received no land. The state subsidised European immigration to replace them, in part with the stated aim of 'whitening' the population.",
 fact="Brazil received about 4.9 million enslaved Africans, more than any other country and roughly 40 per cent of the whole transatlantic trade."),
"la188929": dict(
 bluf="Army officers led by Marshal Manuel Deodoro da Fonseca, with planters angry at uncompensated abolition behind them, set out to replace Brazil's monarchy with a republic. They deposed Brazilian Emperor Pedro II in a nearly bloodless coup on 15 November 1889, and he sailed into exile. The army established itself as the arbiter of Brazilian politics, a role it claimed again in 1930, 1945 and 1964.",
 now="The officers who founded the republic treated the army as guardian of the nation, and that claim justified military interventions from 1889 to the dictatorship of 1964-85. The flag's motto, 'Order and Progress', comes from the positivist philosophy many of them held.",
 story="Brazilian Emperor Pedro II had reigned since 1831, and his heir, Princess Isabel, had lost the planters' loyalty by signing abolition in 1888. On 15 November 1889 Marshal Manuel Deodoro da Fonseca led troops in Rio de Janeiro against the cabinet, and a republic was proclaimed the same day. The imperial family left for Europe within two days.",
 why="Planters who had lost their slaves without compensation in 1888 withdrew their loyalty to the crown. Army officers resented civilian governments that had slighted them since the Paraguayan War. Republican and positivist ideas had spread among junior officers and urban professionals.",
 then="Marshal Deodoro da Fonseca became the first president and resigned in 1891 under military and congressional pressure. Power settled with the coffee oligarchies of São Paulo and Minas Gerais until 1930. The military intervened in politics repeatedly for the next century.",
 fact="Pedro II died in exile in Paris in 1891; he had kept a box of Brazilian soil to be buried with him."),
"la189530": dict(
 bluf="Cuban nationalists led by poet José Martí and General Antonio Maceo rose in 1895 to end Spanish rule after the Ten Years' War had failed to win it. By 1898 they had Spain's army confined to the towns, at the cost of mass civilian deaths in Spanish detention camps. The United States intervened and took the victory, and Cuba gained independence under US supervision, a grievance that fed Cuban nationalism up to 1959.",
 now="Independence on terms written in Washington made sovereignty against the United States the core of Cuban nationalism from 1902. Both the Cuban government and Cuban exiles in Miami claim José Martí as their national hero.",
 story="Cuban poet and organiser José Martí, after years of exile in New York, launched the war in February 1895 with General Antonio Maceo and General Máximo Gómez. Martí was killed at Dos Ríos on 19 May 1895, weeks after landing. From 1896 Spanish Captain-General Valeriano Weyler forced rural civilians into fortified towns, where many died of hunger and disease. Maceo was killed in December 1896.",
 why="Cubans wanted the independence the Ten Years' War of 1868-78 had failed to win. A fall in sugar prices and a US tariff of 1894 ruined planters and workers alike.",
 then="US newspapers publicised the camps, and after USS Maine exploded in Havana in February 1898 the United States declared war on Spain in April. Spain gave up Cuba in December 1898. The Platt Amendment of 1901 gave the United States a right to intervene in independent Cuba.",
 fact="Martí wrote the verses later set to the song 'Guantanamera'."),
"d-maine": dict(
 bluf="The United States sent the battleship Maine to Havana in January 1898 to protect American lives and interests during Cuba's war of independence. It exploded on 15 February, killing 266 of its crew, and a US naval inquiry blamed a mine. Newspapers blamed Spain, Congress declared war in April, and the explosion became the standard case of an unexplained incident carrying a country into war.",
 now="The Maine set the pattern of an incident blamed on an enemy before the evidence was in supplying the case for war. A 1976 US Navy study found the likely cause was a fire in a coal bunker igniting the ship's own magazine.",
 story="The Maine anchored in Havana harbour on 25 January 1898. On the night of 15 February it exploded and sank, killing 266 of its crew. A US naval court of inquiry in March concluded that an external mine had set off the forward magazines, without naming who laid it. 'Remember the Maine! To hell with Spain!' became a newspaper slogan, and Congress declared war in April.",
 why="Washington sent the ship after riots in Havana in January 1898 threatened Americans there. Newspaper publishers William Randolph Hearst and Joseph Pulitzer, competing for readers, had run Spanish atrocity stories for two years.",
 then="The United States defeated Spain in under four months and took Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines. A second inquiry in 1911 also blamed an external explosion; the 1976 study reversed it.",
 fact="The wreck was raised in 1911 and sunk again in deep water in 1912 with military honours."),
"la189831": dict(
 bluf="The United States, having beaten Spain in four months, set out to take Spain's remaining colonies and secure naval bases in the Caribbean and the Pacific. By the Treaty of Paris Spain gave up Cuba and ceded Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines, the last for $20 million. Spain's American empire of four centuries ended, and the United States became an overseas colonial power with Cuba as its protectorate.",
 now="Puerto Rico and Guam have been US territories since 1898; their residents hold US citizenship and cannot vote for president. The Guantánamo lease of 1903 still stands over the Cuban government's objection.",
 story="The treaty was signed in Paris on 10 December 1898. Spain relinquished Cuba and ceded Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines to the United States, which paid $20 million. Cuba became independent in 1902 after accepting the Platt Amendment, which gave the United States a right to intervene and a naval base at Guantánamo Bay.",
 why="Spain had lost both its fleets and could not defend its colonies. US President William McKinley wanted coaling stations and bases for a navy operating in two oceans.",
 then="US troops occupied Cuba until 1902 and returned in 1906-09 and 1917-22. The United States fought Filipino nationalists from 1899 to 1902. The Platt Amendment stood until 1934.",
 fact="Spain called 1898 'the Disaster', and a generation of Spanish writers took the name the Generation of '98."),
"na189856": dict(
 bluf="US President William McKinley went to war with Spain in April 1898 to end the Cuban war on US terms, with the press, sugar interests and naval strategists pressing him. The United States destroyed two Spanish fleets and won in under four months. It became an overseas empire in the Caribbean and the Pacific and began its first counter-insurgency war abroad, in the Philippines.",
 now="The war made the United States a Caribbean and Pacific power from 1898, and the argument it opened over whether a republic should hold colonies runs through every later US debate on intervention. Puerto Rico and Guam remain US territories.",
 story="Congress declared war in April 1898, after USS Maine exploded in Havana harbour in February. US Commodore George Dewey destroyed the Spanish Pacific squadron at Manila Bay on 1 May. Theodore Roosevelt, who had resigned as assistant navy secretary to lead the volunteer Rough Riders, fought at San Juan Hill on 1 July, and the US Navy destroyed the Spanish Atlantic squadron off Santiago de Cuba on 3 July. An armistice followed in August.",
 why="Sympathy for Cuban rebels, stoked by sensational newspapers, gave the war its public support. Investors in Cuban sugar wanted order, and naval strategists wanted bases and coaling stations.",
 trig="The explosion of USS Maine in Havana harbour in February 1898.",
 then="The United States took Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines, made Cuba a protectorate and annexed Hawaii the same year. Filipinos who had fought Spain then fought the United States from 1899 to 1902, a war that killed about 4,200 US soldiers and, by estimates that start at 200,000, far more Filipino civilians.",
 fact="More US soldiers died of disease, mainly typhoid and yellow fever, than of Spanish fire."),
"la190332": dict(
 bluf="US President Theodore Roosevelt wanted a canal across the Isthmus of Panama, and the Colombian Senate rejected his treaty in August 1903. Panamanian separatists declared independence on 3 November and US warships kept Colombian troops from landing. Panama signed the canal treaty Colombia had refused within weeks, and the United States held a zone across a sovereign country until 1999.",
 now="Panama took full control of the canal on 31 December 1999. In 2025 US pressure over Chinese-linked operators at ports near the canal showed it remains a strategic flashpoint between great powers.",
 story="Colombia's Senate rejected the Hay-Herrán canal treaty in August 1903. Panamanian separatists, in contact with French engineer Philippe Bunau-Varilla, rose on 3 November 1903. The gunboat USS Nashville prevented Colombian troops from reaching Panama City. Washington recognised Panama within days, and Bunau-Varilla, as Panama's envoy, signed a treaty on 18 November giving the United States a canal zone in perpetuity.",
 why="The United States wanted a canal under its own control. Colombia's Senate wanted better terms. Panamanians had revolted against rule from Bogotá several times in the 1800s.",
 trig="The Colombian Senate's rejection of the Hay-Herrán Treaty in August 1903.",
 then="The United States controlled the Canal Zone for most of the 20th century. In 1921 it paid Colombia $25 million in what amounted to compensation.",
 fact="Panama's treaty with the United States was signed by a Frenchman, Philippe Bunau-Varilla, before Panama's own delegation reached Washington."),
"la190433": dict(
 bluf="US President Theodore Roosevelt wanted to keep European powers from collecting debts by force in the Americas, after their navies blockaded Venezuela in 1902-03. He claimed a US right to act as 'an international police power' in the hemisphere. The Monroe Doctrine became a licence for US intervention, used to justify occupations of Nicaragua, Haiti and the Dominican Republic over the next three decades.",
 now="The corollary fixed Latin American suspicion of US power from 1904, and that suspicion revives whenever Washington threatens force in the region, as against Venezuela in 2025.",
 story="British, German and Italian warships blockaded Venezuela in 1902-03 over unpaid debts. In his annual message to Congress in December 1904, US President Theodore Roosevelt said 'chronic wrongdoing' in the Americas might force the United States to exercise 'an international police power'. In 1905 the United States took over customs collection in the Dominican Republic to pay its creditors.",
 why="European navies had used force over debts in the hemisphere, and Germany's part in the Venezuela blockade alarmed Washington. The United States was building a canal in Panama and wanted no European base near it.",
 then="US Marines occupied Nicaragua for most of 1912-33, Haiti from 1915 to 1934 and the Dominican Republic from 1916 to 1924. US President Franklin Roosevelt renounced armed intervention under the Good Neighbor policy in 1933.",
 fact="Roosevelt's favourite proverb, 'speak softly and carry a big stick', gave the policy its popular name, the Big Stick."),
"la191034": dict(
 bluf="Mexican landowner Francisco Madero called a revolt in 1910 to end Mexican President Porfirio Díaz's 35-year rule, and peasant armies under Emiliano Zapata and Pancho Villa joined to win land. Díaz fell in 1911, and a decade of civil war killed an estimated 1 to 2 million people. The 1917 constitution promised land, labour rights and national ownership of resources, and the party the winners built ruled Mexico for 71 years.",
 now="The revolution produced Mexico's communal landholdings, the ejidos, and its doctrine of national ownership of oil, and the ruling party it created governed from 1929 to 2000.",
 story="Mexican President Porfirio Díaz had ruled since 1876 with one interruption. After his fraudulent re-election in 1910, Francisco Madero called for revolt on 20 November. Díaz resigned in May 1911 and Madero became president; General Victoriano Huerta overthrew him and had him murdered in February 1913. Constitutionalists under Venustiano Carranza, northern forces under Pancho Villa and southern peasants under Emiliano Zapata, whose cry was 'land and liberty', fought Huerta and then each other. Estimates of the dead from fighting, disease and hunger run from 1 to 2 million.",
 why="Land had concentrated in haciendas under Mexican President Porfirio Díaz, and most rural Mexicans owned none. Rigged elections had closed every legal route to power. Foreign capital owned much of the mines, oil and railways.",
 trig="Díaz's fraudulent re-election in 1910 and Francisco Madero's call to revolt.",
 then="The 1917 constitution promised land reform and national ownership of oil and minerals. Zapata was assassinated in 1919 and Villa in 1923. Mexican President Lázaro Cárdenas redistributed land and nationalised oil in the 1930s. The ruling party, later the PRI, held power until 2000.",
 fact="Revolutionary general Pancho Villa signed a contract with the Mutual Film Company in 1914 to film his battles."),
"la191435": dict(
 bluf="The United States set out to join the Atlantic and Pacific under its own control, where a French company had failed after about 22,000 deaths. It opened the canal on 15 August 1914, cutting the sea route from New York to San Francisco by about 8,000 miles. The United States became a two-ocean naval power, and the Canal Zone became the most visible symbol of US dominance in Latin America until 1999.",
 now="About 5 per cent of world seaborne trade passes through the canal. Drought cut transits in 2023, and US pressure on Panama over Chinese influence in 2025 reopened the question of who controls it.",
 story="A French company led by diplomat Ferdinand de Lesseps began a canal in 1881 and abandoned it after about 22,000 workers died, mostly of malaria and yellow fever. The United States took over in 1904. US Army physician William Gorgas cut the mosquito populations that carried both diseases, and US Army engineer George Goethals built locks that lift ships 26 metres to an artificial lake. The canal opened on 15 August 1914.",
 why="The United States wanted a fast route between its coasts for trade and for its navy. In 1898 the battleship Oregon had needed 67 days to sail round South America to reach Cuba.",
 then="The United States governed a 10-mile-wide Canal Zone through Panama. Riots over the right to fly Panama's flag in the zone killed more than 20 people in January 1964. The 1977 treaties signed by US President Jimmy Carter and Panamanian leader Omar Torrijos returned the canal on 31 December 1999.",
 fact="Ships pay tolls by weight; US writer Richard Halliburton paid 36 cents to swim the canal in 1928."),
"d-constitution1917": dict(
 bluf="Constitutionalist First Chief Venustiano Carranza called a congress to legalise his victory, and the radicals in it set out to give peasants and workers what they had fought for. The constitution proclaimed on 5 February 1917 gave the nation ownership of land and subsoil, licensed land reform and guaranteed labour rights. It became the legal basis for land redistribution and the oil nationalisation of 1938, and a model for social constitutions elsewhere.",
 now="It is still Mexico's constitution, much amended. Article 27's claim of national ownership of land and minerals underpinned the oil nationalisation of 1938 and every later dispute over foreign investment in Mexican energy.",
 story="The Constitutional Congress met at Querétaro and proclaimed the constitution on 5 February 1917. Article 27 gave the nation ownership of land and subsoil resources and allowed land reform. Article 123 guaranteed an eight-hour day and the right to strike. Other articles limited the Church's property and role in education.",
 why="The winning faction under Constitutionalist First Chief Venustiano Carranza needed the peasants and workers who had fought. Radicals in the congress pushed for land and labour rights beyond Carranza's own draft.",
 then="Carranza was overthrown and killed in 1920. Article 27 became the legal basis for land redistribution, which peaked under Mexican President Lázaro Cárdenas in the 1930s, and for nationalising oil in 1938.",
 fact="It wrote social rights such as the eight-hour day into a national constitution before the Soviet constitution of 1918 and the Weimar constitution of 1919."),
"la193036": dict(
 bluf="Rio Grande do Sul Governor Getúlio Vargas led a revolt in 1930 after the Depression wrecked coffee prices and he lost a disputed election. He took power, ruled for 15 years, as dictator from 1937, and built a centralised state with steel mills, labour law and a minimum wage. His model of state-led industry and appeal to urban workers governed Brazil through dictatorship and democracy alike.",
 now="Vargas built the Brazilian state that still exists: labour law, state industry, a centralised bureaucracy and a politics of the urban poor; his model governed Brazil through dictatorship and democracy alike.",
 story="The Depression collapsed coffee prices in 1929, and São Paulo's candidate, Júlio Prestes, won the March 1930 presidential election. Getúlio Vargas, the losing candidate, led a revolt from the south in October 1930 and took power. He imposed the Estado Novo dictatorship in 1937. His government created a minimum wage in 1940, a labour code in 1943 and a national steel company.",
 why="The Depression ruined the coffee economy the old republic rested on. The disputed 1930 election and the murder of Vargas's running mate, Paraíba Governor João Pessoa, gave the opposition its cause.",
 then="The army removed Vargas in October 1945. He won the presidency in 1950 and founded the oil company Petrobras in 1953. Facing a military ultimatum, he shot himself in the presidential palace on 24 August 1954.",
 fact="He is remembered as 'the father of the poor', and his suicide letter ends 'I leave life to enter history'."),
"la193837": dict(
 bluf="Mexican President Lázaro Cárdenas set out to enforce Mexican law on foreign oil companies that had defied the Supreme Court over workers' wages. He expropriated the US and British companies on 18 March 1938 and paid compensation over the following years. Pemex became the emblem of Mexican sovereignty, and the expropriation set the precedent for resource nationalism from Iran to Venezuela and Bolivia.",
 now="Mexico's state oil monopoly ran from 1938 to the energy reform of 2013, and the expropriation set the model that oil producers from Iran in 1951 to Venezuela in 1976 followed.",
 story="Oil workers struck in 1937 for higher wages. A labour board and then Mexico's Supreme Court, on 1 March 1938, ordered the companies to pay. The US and British companies refused. On 18 March 1938 Mexican President Lázaro Cárdenas expropriated them and created Pemex. Mexicans donated jewellery, money and chickens to help pay compensation.",
 why="US and British oil companies defied a Mexican Supreme Court ruling on workers' wages. The 1917 constitution already vested the subsoil in the nation, which gave Cárdenas the legal ground.",
 then="Britain broke off relations, and the companies boycotted Mexican oil. US President Franklin Roosevelt, wanting Mexico's goodwill as war approached, accepted a compensation settlement in 1942. Pemex held Mexico's oil monopoly until 2013.",
 fact="Cárdenas announced the expropriation to the nation by radio on the evening of 18 March 1938."),
"la194638": dict(
 bluf="Argentine Colonel Juan Perón, as labour secretary in the military government, set out to make organised workers his political base. He won the presidency in 1946 on wage rises, pensions and union rights, with his wife Eva Perón as the movement's voice to the poor. Peronism became Argentina's largest political force, and the conflict between Peronists and anti-Peronists organised the country's politics for the rest of the century.",
 now="Peronism has won most of Argentina's free presidential elections since 1946 and remains its largest political force; its mix of welfare, union power and economic nationalism defines the side Argentine politics argues against.",
 story="Argentine Colonel Juan Perón joined the military government of 1943 and, as secretary of labour, raised wages and backed unions. Rivals in the army arrested him in October 1945. Mass demonstrations by workers in Buenos Aires on 17 October freed him. He won the presidential election of February 1946. His wife Eva Perón, 'Evita', ran a social-aid foundation and campaigned for women's suffrage, granted in 1947.",
 why="As labour secretary in a military government, Perón had won workers' loyalty with wage rises and union rights. Mass protests freed him from arrest in 1945 and showed the army that he commanded the street.",
 then="Eva Perón died in 1952. The armed forces overthrew Perón in September 1955 and banned Peronism for 18 years. He returned from exile, won the presidency in 1973 and died in office in July 1974.",
 fact="Eva Perón's embalmed body was taken by the military after 1955 and buried in Milan under a false name for 14 years."),
"la195439": dict(
 bluf="The CIA set out to overthrow Guatemalan President Jacobo Árbenz, whose land reform had taken unused land from the United Fruit Company and whom Washington judged open to communism. An invasion by a few hundred exiles and a radio propaganda campaign forced him to resign in June 1954. Military rule and a 36-year civil war followed, and Latin American leftists concluded that elected reformers would be overthrown by force.",
 now="The coup convinced a generation of Latin American revolutionaries, Argentine doctor Ernesto 'Che' Guevara among them, that reform through elections would be reversed by force. It remains the reference case for Latin American suspicion of US intervention.",
 story="Guatemalan President Jacobo Árbenz, elected in 1950, passed a land reform in 1952 that expropriated uncultivated land, much of it held by the US-owned United Fruit Company, with compensation at the company's own tax valuation. Washington called him a communist threat. In June 1954 a force of exiles under Guatemalan Colonel Carlos Castillo Armas invaded from Honduras with CIA aircraft and radio support. Árbenz resigned on 27 June.",
 why="United Fruit, Guatemala's largest landowner, lobbied Washington against the reform. US President Dwight Eisenhower's administration read Árbenz's tolerance of a small communist party as a Soviet foothold.",
 then="Castillo Armas reversed the land reform. Guatemala's civil war of 1960 to 1996 killed about 200,000 people, most of them Indigenous Maya; a UN-backed truth commission in 1999 attributed 93 per cent of the violations it documented to state forces.",
 fact="The CIA's 'Voice of Liberation' radio broadcast from outside Guatemala while claiming to be inside it."),
"d-granma": dict(
 bluf="Cuban revolutionary Fidel Castro sailed from Mexico with 81 others to start a guerrilla war against Cuban President Fulgencio Batista, in power since a 1952 coup. The landing went badly: the Granma arrived two days late and the army scattered the force within days. The survivors regrouped in the Sierra Maestra and built an army that took power on 1 January 1959, and the landing became the founding act of the Cuban state.",
 now="The landing began the guerrilla war that took Havana in two years and put a Soviet ally ninety miles from Florida; the Cuban revolution's method was copied across Latin America for three decades.",
 story="The overcrowded yacht Granma left Tuxpan, Mexico, with 82 men and landed at Playa Las Coloradas on 2 December 1956, two days late. Cuban President Fulgencio Batista's army ambushed the rebels at Alegría de Pío on 5 December. About 20 survivors, including Fidel Castro, his brother Raúl Castro and Argentine doctor Ernesto 'Che' Guevara, regrouped in the Sierra Maestra mountains.",
 why="Cuban revolutionary Fidel Castro had sworn to overthrow Batista after his failed attack on the Moncada barracks in 1953 and his release in an amnesty in 1955.",
 then="The guerrillas grew into an army as Batista's repression alienated Cubans, and Batista fled on 1 January 1959.",
 fact="The Cuban Communist Party's daily newspaper is named Granma, and so is the province where the boat landed."),
"la195940": dict(
 bluf="Cuban revolutionary Fidel Castro's movement set out to overthrow Cuban President Fulgencio Batista, who had taken power in a 1952 coup. Batista fled on 1 January 1959, and Castro built a one-party socialist state allied with the Soviet Union 90 miles from Florida. Cuba became a Cold War flashpoint and the model for guerrilla movements across Latin America, and the US embargo imposed in 1960-62 remains in force.",
 now="Cuba remains a one-party state in economic crisis, losing population to emigration. The Cuban government and its supporters hold that the revolution delivered universal health care and literacy under a US siege; its opponents hold that it delivered political prisoners, poverty and exile under one-party rule.",
 story="After a failed attack on the Moncada barracks in 1953 and a near-disastrous landing in 1956, Cuban revolutionary Fidel Castro, his brother Raúl Castro and Argentine doctor Ernesto 'Che' Guevara built a guerrilla army in the Sierra Maestra. Cuban President Fulgencio Batista's army lost ground, the United States stopped selling him arms in 1958, and Batista fled on New Year's Day 1959. Castro had promised elections and held none.",
 why="Batista's dictatorship had closed the electoral route after 1952. Inequality between Havana and the countryside was wide, and US companies owned much of the sugar, utilities and mines. Batista's army lost the will to fight in 1958.",
 then="Cuban leader Fidel Castro nationalised US businesses in 1960, and the United States imposed an embargo and broke relations. Cuba allied with the Soviet Union, which led to the missile crisis of 1962. Cuba backed guerrillas in Latin America and sent troops to Angola and Ethiopia, and more than a million Cubans emigrated over the following decades.",
 fact="Cuban intelligence claims there were more than 600 plots to kill Castro. He died of old age in 2016."),
"la196141": dict(
 bluf="The CIA, under plans approved by US President Dwight Eisenhower and carried out under US President John F. Kennedy, landed about 1,400 Cuban exiles to overthrow Cuban leader Fidel Castro. Castro's forces defeated them in three days and captured most of them. Castro emerged stronger, Cuba moved openly into the Soviet camp, and the Soviet decision to place missiles in Cuba followed the next year.",
 now="The invasion made Castro's rule secure and his Soviet alliance open from 1961, and it is the standard case of a covert action that strengthens its target and pushes it toward a rival.",
 story="Brigade 2506, about 1,400 Cuban exiles trained by the CIA in Guatemala, landed at the Bay of Pigs on 17 April 1961. Air strikes two days earlier had left part of Cuba's air force intact, and US President John F. Kennedy cancelled a second strike and refused direct air support. Cuban leader Fidel Castro's forces sealed the beachhead, and by 19 April most of the brigade had been captured.",
 why="Washington wanted Cuban leader Fidel Castro removed after he nationalised US property and moved toward Moscow. Planners assumed a landing would spark a popular uprising, and Kennedy wanted US involvement deniable.",
 then="Castro declared the revolution socialist on 16 April, the eve of the landing, and himself a Marxist-Leninist in December 1961. The prisoners were ransomed in December 1962 for $53 million in food and medicine. The Soviet Union placed nuclear missiles in Cuba in 1962.",
 fact="Kennedy said afterwards that 'victory has a hundred fathers and defeat is an orphan'."),
"la196242": dict(
 bluf="Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev placed nuclear missiles in Cuba to protect Cuban leader Fidel Castro and offset US missiles in Turkey; US President John F. Kennedy set out to remove them without war. After 13 days of blockade and bargaining, Khrushchev withdrew them for a public US pledge not to invade Cuba and a secret promise to remove the missiles in Turkey. Both powers then built tools for managing the nuclear rivalry, and détente followed.",
 now="The crisis produced the first instruments of arms control, the hotline and the test-ban treaty of 1963, and it is the reference case for nuclear crisis diplomacy from Ukraine to Taiwan.",
 story="In October 1962 US spy planes found Soviet nuclear missile sites under construction in Cuba. US President John F. Kennedy declared a naval 'quarantine' of the island on 22 October. A US spy plane was shot down over Cuba on 27 October, and a Soviet submarine came close to firing a nuclear torpedo. On 28 October Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev agreed to remove the missiles in exchange for a US pledge not to invade Cuba and a secret promise to remove US missiles from Turkey.",
 why="Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev wanted to protect Cuban leader Fidel Castro after the Bay of Pigs. He also wanted to offset US missiles in Turkey and Italy and the US lead in long-range missiles.",
 trig="US U-2 photographs of the missile sites, taken on 14 October 1962.",
 then="Both sides set up a hotline and signed a partial test-ban treaty in 1963. The Soviet Union began a nuclear build-up to end its inferiority. Khrushchev was ousted in 1964.",
 fact="The Moscow-Washington hotline set up in 1963 was a teletype link, never a red telephone."),
"la196443": dict(
 bluf="Brazil's officers, business leaders and the US government feared that Brazilian President João Goulart's land and tax reforms and his ties to the left would bring communism. The army removed him on 31 March-1 April 1964 and ruled for 21 years. It was the first of the long South American military regimes of the 1960s and 1970s, and Uruguay, Chile and Argentina followed.",
 now="The 1979 amnesty shielded the regime's officers, and the military's claim to guard the nation outlasted the dictatorship. In 2025 Brazil's Supreme Federal Court convicted former President Jair Bolsonaro, who had praised the dictatorship, of plotting a coup after losing the 2022 election; his supporters call the case political persecution, and his opponents call it the first time Brazil has punished a coup plot.",
 story="Brazilian President João Goulart had taken office in 1961 and proposed land reform, limits on foreign profit remittances and votes for illiterates. On 31 March 1964 army units in Minas Gerais rebelled, and Goulart left for Uruguay within days. The US government had a naval task force ready to support the plotters if fighting broke out. Marshal Humberto Castelo Branco took office as president on 15 April.",
 why="Officers, landowners and business leaders read Goulart's reforms and his ties to unions as a turn toward communism. The US government, after Cuba, feared a second revolution in Latin America's largest country and encouraged the plotters.",
 then="Institutional Act No. 5 of December 1968 closed Congress and suspended habeas corpus, and torture of political prisoners became routine. The economy grew about 10 per cent a year in 1968-73. Civilian rule returned in 1985. Brazil's National Truth Commission counted 434 people killed or disappeared by the regime in its 2014 report.",
 fact="The regime kept Congress open for most of its 21 years and held elections between two licensed parties."),
"la196744": dict(
 bluf="Argentine-born revolutionary Ernesto 'Che' Guevara went to Bolivia in 1966 to start a guerrilla war that would spread across South America. Bolivian peasants stayed away, and Bolivian troops advised by the CIA captured him on 8 October 1967 and shot him the next day. His strategy failed, and his death made him the most reproduced revolutionary image of the century.",
 now="Che's failure in Bolivia discredited the rural guerrilla model in South America after 1967, and Cuba turned to sending its own troops abroad, above all to Africa from 1975.",
 story="Argentine-born revolutionary Ernesto 'Che' Guevara left Cuba in 1965, fought in the Congo and arrived in Bolivia in November 1966 with a small band of Cuban and Bolivian fighters. Bolivian peasants gave him little support, and the Bolivian Communist Party declined to back him. Bolivian Rangers trained by US advisers captured him at Quebrada del Yuro on 8 October 1967. He was executed the next day in the schoolhouse at La Higuera on the Bolivian government's orders.",
 why="Che believed a small guerrilla band could create the conditions for revolution. Bolivia's peasants had received land in the 1953 reform and had little reason to join foreign insurgents.",
 then="The Bolivian army displayed his body to the press in Vallegrande and buried it in secret. His remains were found in 1997 and reburied in Santa Clara, Cuba. Cuba shifted from sponsoring guerrillas to sending its own troops, especially to Angola.",
 fact="Cuban photographer Alberto Korda's portrait of Che, taken in 1960, is often called the most reproduced photograph in the world."),
"la197345": dict(
 bluf="Chilean General Augusto Pinochet and the armed forces, with US encouragement, set out to remove Chilean President Salvador Allende's elected socialist government amid economic crisis and political deadlock. They bombed the presidential palace on 11 September 1973, and Allende died inside. Seventeen years of military rule followed, in which official commissions counted more than 3,000 killed or disappeared, and Chile became a laboratory for free-market reform.",
 now="The 1980 constitution written under Pinochet still governs Chile; voters rejected replacement drafts in 2022 and 2023. Pinochet's defenders hold that the coup stopped a Marxist government and that his reforms made Chile one of Latin America's richest countries; his opponents hold that it destroyed a democracy and that the growth rested on repression and inequality.",
 story="Chilean President Salvador Allende, a socialist, won the 1970 election with 36.6 per cent of the vote. His government nationalised copper in 1971 and expropriated farms and firms; by 1973 inflation ran in the hundreds of per cent and goods were short. In August 1973 the Chamber of Deputies passed a resolution accusing the government of breaking the constitution. On 11 September 1973 the armed forces under General Augusto Pinochet bombed La Moneda palace, and Allende died inside, by his own hand, as a forensic inquiry confirmed in 2011.",
 why="Allende's supporters hold that US economic pressure and covert funding of the opposition, ordered by US President Richard Nixon from 1970, wrecked the economy; his opponents hold that his own expropriations, price controls and money printing did. Cold War fears in Washington and in Chile's officer corps turned the crisis into a coup.",
 then="Official commissions of 1991 and 2011 counted about 3,200 killed or disappeared and more than 38,000 tortured or imprisoned for political reasons. From 1975 Chilean officials trained at the University of Chicago privatised pensions and state firms and opened trade. Pinochet lost a plebiscite in 1988 and handed power to an elected president in 1990. He was arrested in London in 1998 on a Spanish warrant and died in 2006 without being convicted.",
 fact="The coup took place on 11 September 1973, and Chileans call it their own 9/11."),
"la197646": dict(
 bluf="Argentina's military junta seized power on 24 March 1976 to destroy left-wing guerrillas and anyone it judged subversive. It kidnapped, tortured and killed thousands in secret detention centres until defeat in the Falklands brought it down in 1983. Argentina became the first country in the region to try a former junta in civilian courts, in 1985, and the count of the disappeared remains a political fight.",
 now="Trials of officers resumed in 2006 after the amnesty laws were struck down, and the Grandmothers of the Plaza de Mayo still identify children stolen from detainees through DNA. Argentina's truth commission documented 8,961 disappearances in 1984 and human rights organisations put the total at 30,000; Argentine President Javier Milei's government treats 30,000 as an inflated symbolic figure, and the human rights groups treat that challenge as denial.",
 story="Argentina's armed forces, led by Argentine General Jorge Rafael Videla, overthrew Argentine President Isabel Perón on 24 March 1976. The junta kidnapped students, union activists and suspected leftists, held them in hundreds of secret detention centres and tortured most of them. Many were drugged and thrown alive from aircraft into the sea. Babies born in captivity were given to military families.",
 why="Defenders of the armed forces hold that they fought a war against guerrillas, the Montoneros and the ERP, who had killed hundreds in kidnappings and bombings; the human rights movement holds that the state turned terror on a population whose guerrillas were largely beaten by 1976. The officers adopted a doctrine that treated political opponents of every kind as enemies in an internal war.",
 then="The Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo began marching outside the presidential palace in April 1977. Defeat in the Falklands in 1982 broke the junta, and elected Argentine President Raúl Alfonsín took office in December 1983. The Trial of the Juntas convicted Videla and others in 1985; amnesty laws and pardons followed in 1986-90 and were annulled in 2003-05.",
 fact="The Mothers walk in a circle because police forbade them to stand together in groups."),
"la197947": dict(
 bluf="The Sandinista National Liberation Front set out to overthrow Nicaraguan President Anastasio Somoza Debayle, whose family had ruled with US support since the 1930s. A national uprising drove Somoza out on 17 July 1979, and the Sandinistas took power. The United States funded the Contra rebels against them, Nicaragua became a Cold War battleground, and Sandinista leader Daniel Ortega has ruled again since 2007.",
 now="Nicaraguan President Daniel Ortega has governed since 2007, since 2025 as co-president with his wife Rosario Murillo. Opponents and UN investigators describe the jailing and exile of rivals since the 2018 protests as authoritarian rule; the government describes the protests as a foreign-backed coup attempt.",
 story="The Somoza family had ruled Nicaragua since the 1930s with US support. After the 1972 Managua earthquake much of the foreign aid was diverted by the regime. The murder of opposition editor Pedro Joaquín Chamorro in January 1978 set off strikes and revolts. A popular uprising led by the Sandinista guerrillas spread through 1978-79, and Nicaraguan President Anastasio Somoza Debayle fled on 17 July 1979. The Sandinistas entered Managua two days later.",
 why="Four decades of family rule had closed every peaceful route to power. The theft of earthquake aid after 1972 and the killing of Chamorro united business leaders and the Church with the guerrillas. US President Jimmy Carter's administration withdrew its support from Somoza.",
 then="US President Ronald Reagan funded the Contra rebels from 1981 in a war that killed about 30,000. The Iran-Contra scandal of 1986 exposed secret funding through arms sales to Iran. Ortega lost the 1990 election to Violeta Chamorro, the murdered editor's widow, and returned to power in 2007.",
 fact="Somoza fled to Paraguay and was assassinated in Asunción in 1980 by an Argentine guerrilla commando."),
"la198248": dict(
 bluf="Argentine President General Leopoldo Galtieri invaded the British-held Falkland Islands in April 1982 to win popularity amid economic crisis, expecting Britain to accept it. British Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher sent a task force and retook the islands by 14 June, at a cost of about 650 Argentine and 255 British lives. Defeat brought down the junta and restored democracy in Argentina, and victory secured Thatcher's re-election in 1983.",
 now="Argentina's claim to the islands it calls the Malvinas is written into its 1994 constitution, and islanders voted 99.8 per cent to remain British in 2013; the dispute keeps relations between the two countries cool.",
 story="Facing economic crisis and protest, Argentina's military government landed forces on the British-ruled islands on 2 April 1982. British Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher sent a task force 13,000 km. The British submarine Conqueror sank the cruiser General Belgrano on 2 May. British troops landed in May and took the surrender at Stanley on 14 June. About 650 Argentines and 255 British servicemen died.",
 why="Argentina's military government, facing economic crisis and protests, hoped a patriotic victory over islands it had long claimed would rescue its popularity. It expected Britain to protest and accept the result.",
 then="Argentine President Leopoldo Galtieri resigned within days of the surrender. Argentina returned to elected government in December 1983. Thatcher won a large majority in June 1983.",
 fact="The sinking of the General Belgrano killed 323 men, almost half of all Argentine deaths in the war."),
"la198248b": dict(
 bluf="Latin American governments had borrowed heavily from Western banks in the 1970s to fund state-led development. When US interest rates rose to record highs, Mexico announced in August 1982 that it could not service its debt, and banks stopped lending across the region. A 'lost decade' of stagnation and IMF-imposed austerity followed, and the region abandoned state-led industrialisation for the market reforms called the Washington Consensus.",
 now="The crisis ended state-led industrialisation in Latin America in the 1980s and made IMF conditions the price of rescue; Argentina's repeated defaults and IMF programmes show the cycle continuing.",
 story="Oil exporters deposited their surpluses in Western banks in the 1970s, and the banks lent heavily to Latin American governments. US Federal Reserve Chairman Paul Volcker raised interest rates to fight inflation from 1979, and the cost of the debt soared. In August 1982 Mexico announced it could not service its foreign debt. Banks stopped lending across the region, and country after country turned to the IMF for loans conditioned on austerity.",
 why="Banks recycled oil exporters' surpluses into loans to Latin America in the 1970s at floating rates. When US interest rates soared to fight inflation, the debts became unpayable.",
 trig="Mexico's announcement in August 1982 that it could not service its debts.",
 then="Income per head in Latin America fell through most of the 1980s. US Treasury Secretary Nicholas Brady's plan of 1989 swapped bank loans for bonds with partial write-downs and ended the crisis. Privatisation and trade liberalisation spread across the region in the 1990s.",
 fact="The term 'Washington Consensus' was coined in 1989 for ten policies the IMF, the World Bank and the US Treasury urged on the region."),
"la198949": dict(
 bluf="US President George H. W. Bush set out to remove Panamanian military ruler Manuel Noriega, a former CIA asset indicted in the United States for drug trafficking. About 26,000 US troops invaded on 20 December 1989, and Noriega surrendered on 3 January 1990. It was the last large-scale US invasion in Latin America and showed that, with the Cold War ending, Washington faced no Soviet check on force in its own hemisphere.",
 now="The invasion is the precedent for US military action to seize a foreign leader indicted on US drug charges, and it was cited in debates over US action against the government of Venezuela from 2020.",
 story="Panamanian military ruler Manuel Noriega had worked with the CIA for years and was indicted in Florida in 1988 for drug trafficking. He annulled the May 1989 election his opponents had won. After Panamanian forces killed a US Marine officer at a checkpoint on 16 December, 26,000 US troops invaded on 20 December. Noriega took refuge in the Vatican embassy and surrendered on 3 January 1990.",
 why="US President George H. W. Bush cited drug trafficking, the killing of a Marine, the annulled election and the safety of the canal.",
 then="Civilian deaths are disputed: the US military counted about 200, and Panamanian and human rights groups estimated hundreds to several thousand. Noriega was convicted in Miami in 1992. Panama abolished its army.",
 fact="US troops played loud rock music outside the Vatican embassy to pressure Noriega to surrender."),
"la199450": dict(
 bluf="The Zapatista Army of National Liberation, a movement of Indigenous Maya peasants in Chiapas, rose on 1 January 1994, the day NAFTA took effect, to demand land, autonomy and an end to their exclusion. Twelve days of fighting ended in a ceasefire and talks. The rebels won national attention and self-governing communities, and their use of media made them a model for the global anti-globalisation movement.",
 now="The uprising put Indigenous rights on Mexico's national agenda and produced the constitutional reform of 2001. Zapatista communities in Chiapas have governed themselves outside state institutions since 2003.",
 story="On 1 January 1994, the day NAFTA took effect, the masked Zapatista Army of National Liberation seized towns in Chiapas, Mexico's poorest state. Its spokesman, Subcomandante Marcos, issued communiqués to the national and foreign press. After 12 days of fighting the government declared a ceasefire, and talks followed.",
 why="Indigenous poverty and land loss came first. A 1992 constitutional change ended land redistribution, and the rebels feared NAFTA's cheap US maize would ruin small farmers.",
 then="The San Andrés Accords on Indigenous rights were signed in 1996. Paramilitaries killed 45 people at Acteal in December 1997. Congress passed a narrower Indigenous-rights reform in 2001, which the Zapatistas rejected.",
 fact="The government identified Subcomandante Marcos in 1995 as Rafael Guillén, a former university lecturer in design."),
"la199951": dict(
 bluf="Former paratrooper Hugo Chávez won Venezuela's presidency in December 1998 promising to sweep away the two parties that had shared power for 40 years. He rewrote the constitution, spent oil revenue on programmes for the poor and brought the courts, the state oil company and much of the media under his control. His government led Latin America's turn to the left in the 2000s, and the oil dependence and weakened institutions he left preceded Venezuela's collapse after 2014.",
 now="The constitution and movement Venezuelan President Hugo Chávez built from 1999 have kept his party in power since. His supporters hold that he gave the poor a share of oil wealth and a political voice; his opponents hold that he dismantled checks on power and left an economy that collapsed under his successor.",
 story="Hugo Chávez, a former army officer who had led a failed coup in 1992, won Venezuela's presidential election in December 1998, promising to sweep away a corrupt two-party system. In office from 1999 he wrote a new constitution and, as oil prices rose, spent heavily on programmes for the poor, while taking control of the courts, the state oil company and much of the media. He won repeated elections until his death in 2013.",
 why="Venezuelan incomes had fallen since the oil price collapse of the 1980s, and the two traditional parties were discredited by corruption. Chávez's 1992 coup attempt had made him the best-known opponent of the system.",
 then="A coup removed Chávez for two days in April 2002 before loyal troops restored him. Leftist leaders won across the region in the 2000s, from Bolivia and Ecuador to Brazil and Argentina, financed partly by the commodity boom. Chávez died in March 2013 and had named Nicolás Maduro his successor.",
 fact="Chávez hosted a live Sunday television programme, 'Aló Presidente', that sometimes ran for more than six hours."),
"la200152": dict(
 bluf="Argentina had pegged the peso one-to-one to the dollar since 1991 to end hyperinflation, and borrowed abroad to sustain it. By late 2001 recession and debt broke the peg; banks froze deposits, riots forced Argentine President Fernando de la Rúa out, and the country defaulted on close to $100 billion, then the largest sovereign default on record. Poverty passed 50 per cent, and distrust of the peso and of the IMF became fixed features of Argentine politics.",
 now="Argentina has defaulted nine times, and the 2001 crisis framed its politics from the Kirchner governments, which ran against IMF austerity, to Argentine President Javier Milei, who ran on dollarisation.",
 story="Argentina pegged its peso to the US dollar in 1991. A recession began in 1998, and foreign debt grew. In December 2001 the government froze bank deposits, the 'corralito', and riots broke out. Argentine President Fernando de la Rúa resigned on 20 December, the country declared default days later, and five presidents held office in about two weeks. The peg was abandoned in January 2002.",
 why="The peg made exports uncompetitive once Brazil devalued in 1999 and the dollar rose. Federal and provincial deficits were financed by foreign borrowing. The IMF withheld a loan payment in December 2001.",
 then="The peso lost about three-quarters of its value against the dollar in 2002, and poverty passed 50 per cent. Argentine President Néstor Kirchner restructured the debt at a deep discount in 2005 and paid off the IMF in 2006. Holdout creditors litigated in New York until 2016.",
 fact="Middle-class protesters banging pots and pans, the 'cacerolazo', became the sound of the crisis."),
"la200353": dict(
 bluf="Former metalworker and union leader Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva won Brazil's presidency in 2002 promising to cut poverty without breaking the markets that feared him. He kept orthodox fiscal policy, expanded cash transfers through Bolsa Família and rode a commodity boom, and millions left poverty. Corruption scandals later engulfed his Workers' Party, and the fight over his record defined Brazil's left-right polarisation.",
 now="Lula won the presidency again in 2022 after a corruption conviction that was later annulled; the contest between his camp and Bolsonaro's has organised Brazilian politics since 2018.",
 story="Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, who had lost three presidential elections, won in October 2002 and took office on 1 January 2003. His government launched Bolsa Família in 2003, paying poor families monthly cash on condition that their children attended school and were vaccinated. High prices for soy, iron ore and oil financed rising wages and spending. He left office at the end of 2010 with approval above 80 per cent.",
 why="Disappointment with the market reforms of the 1990s came first. Deep inequality and the appeal of a former metalworker who had led strikes against the military regime did the rest.",
 then="The 'mensalão' vote-buying scandal of 2005 and the Lava Jato investigation from 2014 reached his party. Lula was convicted of corruption in 2017 and jailed in 2018; the Supreme Federal Court annulled the convictions in 2021. He defeated Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro in 2022.",
 fact="Bolsa Família cost about 0.5 per cent of GDP."),
"la201054": dict(
 bluf="A magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck near Port-au-Prince on 12 January 2010, in the poorest country in the Americas. Estimates of the dead run from about 100,000 to more than 200,000. Billions of dollars in pledged aid produced little lasting reconstruction, UN peacekeepers introduced cholera, and the weakening of the Haitian state set up the collapse that followed after 2021.",
 now="Haiti never recovered, and the collapse of its state after 2021, with gangs controlling most of the capital, has roots in the failures of reconstruction.",
 story="On 12 January 2010 a magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck near Port-au-Prince. Poorly built concrete buildings collapsed, including the National Palace and the headquarters of the UN mission. Estimates of the dead range from about 100,000 to over 200,000.",
 why="Haiti lies on a major fault, and poverty meant few buildings were built to resist earthquakes.",
 then="Donors pledged about $10 billion, much of it spent through foreign contractors and agencies. Cholera brought by UN peacekeepers from October 2010 killed about 10,000 Haitians; the UN acknowledged its role in 2016.",
 fact="The head of the UN mission in Haiti, Tunisian diplomat Hédi Annabi, died when the mission's headquarters collapsed."),
"la201455": dict(
 bluf="Venezuelan President Nicolás Maduro set out to keep his government's programmes and control after oil fell from over $100 a barrel in mid-2014, and kept spending, printing money and controlling prices. The economy shrank by about three-quarters, hyperinflation wiped out wages, and nearly 8 million people, about a quarter of the population, left. The exodus reshaped migration across the region, and Venezuela became a target of US sanctions and military pressure.",
 now="UN figures for 2024 put Venezuelans abroad at nearly 8 million, one of the world's largest displacement crises, and in 2025 the United States built up naval forces in the Caribbean against Maduro's government. The government blames the collapse on US sanctions and economic war; its opponents blame mismanagement, expropriation and corruption that began before the sanctions.",
 story="Oil supplied about 95 per cent of Venezuela's export earnings. When prices fell from over $100 a barrel in mid-2014 to under $30 in early 2016, the government kept spending, printed money and tightened price controls. The economy shrank by roughly three-quarters over the following years, hyperinflation wiped out wages and savings, and food and medicine ran short.",
 why="Oil provided almost all export earnings, and price controls, expropriations and corruption had already cut output at the state oil company. US financial sanctions from 2017 and oil sanctions from 2019 cut access to credit and markets.",
 then="Nearly 8 million Venezuelans left. Maduro claimed victory in disputed elections in 2018 and 2024; in 2024 the electoral council declared him the winner without publishing a breakdown, and the opposition published tally sheets it said showed its candidate, Edmundo González, had won. The United States imposed sweeping sanctions.",
 fact="Venezuela's central bank put inflation in 2018 at about 130,000 per cent."),
"la201656": dict(
 bluf="Colombian President Juan Manuel Santos and the FARC guerrillas negotiated for four years to end a war that had run since 1964. Voters narrowly rejected the first deal in October 2016; a revised version passed Congress in November, and about 13,000 FARC members disarmed. Latin America's longest insurgency ended as an armed force, while other armed groups expanded into the territory the FARC left.",
 now="The deal turned the FARC into a political party in 2017 and created a transitional court for war crimes; dissident factions and other armed groups have expanded into former FARC areas since.",
 story="After four years of talks in Havana, Colombian President Juan Manuel Santos and the FARC signed a peace deal in September 2016 to end a war that had lasted since 1964. Voters narrowly rejected it in a referendum on 2 October. A revised deal was approved by Congress in November.",
 why="A decade of military pressure under the US-funded Plan Colombia had killed FARC commanders and cut its numbers by more than half. Both sides saw no path to victory.",
 then="Santos won the Nobel Peace Prize in October 2016. About 13,000 FARC members disarmed and the group became a political party. Hundreds of former fighters and social leaders were later killed, and dissident factions rearmed.",
 fact="Colombia's truth commission estimated in 2022 that about 450,000 people were killed in the conflict between 1985 and 2018."),
"la202157": dict(
 bluf="Haitian President Jovenel Moïse was ruling by decree, with no sitting parliament and the end of his term disputed, when a commando that included more than 20 former Colombian soldiers killed him at home on 7 July 2021. Who ordered the killing remains unresolved. Haiti was left without a single elected national official, and armed gangs took control of most of Port-au-Prince.",
 now="The collapse of the state since 2021 has left most of the capital governed by gangs; UN figures for 2025 put the displaced at more than a million and about half the population facing acute hunger.",
 story="On 7 July 2021 a commando unit that included more than 20 former Colombian soldiers stormed Haitian President Jovenel Moïse's home in Port-au-Prince and shot him dead.",
 why="Haiti was in deep political crisis: Haitian President Jovenel Moïse had been ruling by decree, and the end of his term was disputed. Who ordered the killing remains unclear.",
 then="Haiti was left without a single elected national official. Gangs took over most of Port-au-Prince. A Kenyan-led international mission arrived in 2024, and in 2025 the UN authorised a larger force to fight the gangs.",
 fact="Several suspects were tried in a US federal court in Miami, where some pleaded guilty."),
"la202358": dict(
 bluf="Libertarian commentator Javier Milei campaigned with a chainsaw to cut the Argentine state and end inflation above 140 per cent a year. He won the November 2023 run-off with about 56 per cent of the vote, cut spending sharply and produced budget surpluses and lower monthly inflation while poverty rose at first. His government is a test of whether rapid austerity can break Argentina's cycle of crises while keeping public support.",
 now="His party won the October 2025 midterm elections after a US-backed financial rescue, strengthening his hand for further reform. Supporters hold that he ended the deficits and inflation every predecessor tolerated; opponents hold that he cut pensions, universities and public works and that the poor paid for the stabilisation.",
 story="Javier Milei, a libertarian TV commentator trained in economics, campaigned with a chainsaw as the symbol of cutting the state. He won Argentina's presidential run-off in November 2023 with about 56 per cent of the vote, as annual inflation topped 140 per cent.",
 why="Runaway inflation and rising poverty came first. Anger at the established parties, Peronist and anti-Peronist alike, gave an outsider his opening.",
 then="Argentine President Javier Milei devalued the peso by more than half in December 2023 and cut public spending sharply. Argentina ran its first annual budget surplus since 2010 in 2024, and monthly inflation fell from 25 per cent in December 2023 to low single digits. The official poverty rate rose above 50 per cent in early 2024 and fell below 40 per cent by the end of the year. The United States backed the peso with a $20 billion currency swap in October 2025.",
 fact="In his first week Milei cut the number of ministries from 18 to 9."),
}
